  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nIssued 210 PM MST SUN NOV 15 2026\nThe brief high pressure ridge over the area will quickly break \ndown by Wednesday afternoon, as a closed Pacific upper low \nsteers into California and the Great Basin. This system will \nbring a more prolonged precipitation event from Wednesday \nthrough the end of the week. Guidance continues to suggest the \ncore of the low digging south of our area, steering abundant \nwraparound moisture and continuous precipitation over our area \nthrough late Friday. Continuous valley rain showers and mountain\nsnow are expected Wednesday through late Friday. Lower valley \nareas are looking at a couple hundredths of an inch up to 0.2 \ninches of total rainfall. Latest long-range guidance has snow \nlevels hanging in the 3500-4500 ft range through Saturday. \nValleys can see a rain/snow mix, mainly on Friday. Higher \nmountain peaks can still see possible snow accumulations of 5 to\n10 inches total by early Saturday. Not much cooling is expected\nwith this system, and temperatures are expected to hang near or\nslightly below seasonal normals through Friday. Come early \nSaturday, the system will steer out of our area as a ridge \nbuilds back in. Saturday and Sunday will generally see slightly \nwarmer temperatures and relatively drier conditions. Another \nPacific trough is projected to steer into the region, but \nconsiderable uncertainty remains over its precipitation timing \nand intensity. For now, late Saturday through early Sunday will \nsee slight chances of precipitation mainly over higher terrain \nareas.",
